WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
So in exactly 13 months, a multi-billion-dollar trap door

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:07.520
is going to just completely open up

00:00:07.520 --> 00:00:10.360
underneath the American health care system.

00:00:10.360 --> 00:00:12.640
And the craziest part about this whole thing,

00:00:12.640 --> 00:00:15.360
the United States government actually built this trap door

00:00:15.360 --> 00:00:17.640
entirely on purpose.

00:00:17.640 --> 00:00:18.640
Yeah, they really did.

00:00:18.640 --> 00:00:21.800
I mean, it is hard-coded right into the statutory math.

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:23.200
And when it opens, it's basically

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:25.600
designed to drop the floor out from under the most

00:00:25.600 --> 00:00:27.440
vulnerable people in the entire system.

00:00:27.440 --> 00:00:28.120
Right.

00:00:28.160 --> 00:00:30.000
So welcome to this deep dive.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.800
Today is September 2, 2026.

00:00:32.800 --> 00:00:35.080
And we're looking at this massive ticking clock

00:00:35.080 --> 00:00:38.080
that ends on October 1, 2027.

00:00:38.080 --> 00:00:41.000
And for everyone listening, we want to make it clear

00:00:41.000 --> 00:00:43.440
that this isn't just some abstract policy debate

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:45.760
about decimal points in a basement committee room

00:00:45.760 --> 00:00:46.080
somewhere.

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:47.200
Absolutely not, no.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:49.920
This trap door specifically, and really disproportionately,

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:54.040
targets 1.3 million American citizens living in Puerto Rico.

00:00:54.040 --> 00:00:54.540
Yeah.

00:00:54.540 --> 00:00:56.800
And just to give you a sense of the scale here,

00:00:56.800 --> 00:00:59.800
that is roughly 47% of the entire population of the island.

00:00:59.800 --> 00:01:00.920
Wow.

00:01:00.920 --> 00:01:01.800
Half the island.

00:01:01.800 --> 00:01:02.840
Almost half, yeah.

00:01:02.840 --> 00:01:05.320
Almost half of Puerto Rico relies on the local Medicaid

00:01:05.320 --> 00:01:08.320
program, which is known down there as Plan Vital,

00:01:08.320 --> 00:01:11.400
for their most basic physical and behavioral health needs.

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:15.520
And right now, their access to a doctor, to a hospital bed,

00:01:15.520 --> 00:01:18.960
to life-saving medication, it's hanging

00:01:18.960 --> 00:01:21.840
by a very, very frayed legislative threat.

00:01:21.840 --> 00:01:23.440
It's terrifying, honestly.

00:01:23.440 --> 00:01:25.840
So my goal today is I really want

00:01:25.840 --> 00:01:27.800
to figure out how we got here.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:30.160
And more importantly, how the people who

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.320
are trying to stop this trap door from opening

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:34.040
are actually planning to do it.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.320
Because we have this massive stack of documents

00:01:36.320 --> 00:01:37.360
in front of us today.

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:39.360
A literal mountain of paperwork.

00:01:39.360 --> 00:01:40.120
Literally.

00:01:40.120 --> 00:01:43.240
We are looking at these highly technical congressional research

00:01:43.240 --> 00:01:44.040
service reports.

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.320
We've got executive orders from the Puerto Rican government.

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:47.760
We're looking at certified budgets

00:01:47.760 --> 00:01:50.160
from financial oversight boards and just hundreds

00:01:50.160 --> 00:01:52.480
of pages of Medicaid annual reports.

00:01:52.480 --> 00:01:54.400
And our mission today is to, well,

00:01:54.400 --> 00:01:58.080
extract the actual signal from all that bureaucratic noise.

00:01:58.080 --> 00:02:02.040
Because underneath all that really dry bureaucratic language,

00:02:02.040 --> 00:02:05.760
I mean, this is fundamentally a story about unequal rules.

00:02:05.760 --> 00:02:09.400
It's about the heavy burden of having to constantly prove

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:11.920
you deserve the funding that you're asking for.

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:15.920
And it's about the relentless, unforgiving nature

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:17.720
of congressional deadlines.

00:02:17.720 --> 00:02:20.840
It is just this profound look at what

00:02:20.840 --> 00:02:24.800
happens when the math that governs human lives

00:02:24.800 --> 00:02:28.280
is written differently for one specific group of citizens

00:02:28.280 --> 00:02:30.640
than it is for literally everyone else.

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:31.120
Yeah.

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:33.760
So before we really get into the impending cliff,

00:02:33.760 --> 00:02:36.840
I think we need to understand the baseline, like how

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:38.400
this works for most people.

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:40.120
Because if you live in one of the 50 states,

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:43.440
Medicaid works in a very specific kind of invisible way.

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:45.680
You probably don't even think about the funding mechanism

00:02:45.680 --> 00:02:47.400
if you or a family member needs it, right?

00:02:47.400 --> 00:02:49.480
Most people definitely do not think about it now.

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:52.440
So how is the system actually supposed to function normally?

00:02:52.440 --> 00:02:55.880
OK, so in the 50 states and in the District of Columbia

00:02:55.880 --> 00:02:59.280
too, Medicaid funding is what we call open-ended.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.960
It's an entitlement program with a guaranteed federal matching

00:03:01.960 --> 00:03:02.480
structure.

00:03:02.480 --> 00:03:03.000
Meaning what?

00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:03.720
Exactly.

00:03:03.720 --> 00:03:07.000
So if an individual meets the eligibility requirements

00:03:07.000 --> 00:03:10.960
and they need medical care, the state provides that care.

00:03:10.960 --> 00:03:13.600
The state pays the doctor or the hospital.

00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:15.400
And then the federal government guarantees

00:03:15.400 --> 00:03:17.520
that they will reimburse the state

00:03:17.560 --> 00:03:21.160
for a specific percentage of that cost, always.

00:03:21.160 --> 00:03:23.640
So the state never has to worry that the federal government

00:03:23.640 --> 00:03:27.040
is suddenly going to say, sorry, we're out of money for the year.

00:03:27.040 --> 00:03:28.320
You're totally on your own.

00:03:28.320 --> 00:03:28.960
Never.

00:03:28.960 --> 00:03:31.440
The federal matching funds are totally guaranteed.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:34.320
And that federal share, it's determined by a formula

00:03:34.320 --> 00:03:36.560
called the Federal Medical Assistance Percentage.

00:03:36.560 --> 00:03:38.000
OK, the FMAP.

00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:39.840
Right, you will hear it referred to constantly

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:42.360
in policy circles as the FMAP.

00:03:42.360 --> 00:03:45.360
Now, the FMAP ranges from a minimum of 50%

00:03:45.360 --> 00:03:48.440
all the way up to a maximum of 83%.

00:03:48.440 --> 00:03:51.360
And the way they decide where a specific state falls

00:03:51.360 --> 00:03:55.120
on that scale is strictly based on the state's per capita income.

00:03:55.120 --> 00:03:57.320
OK, meaning the poorer states get more help

00:03:57.320 --> 00:03:58.560
from the federal government.

00:03:58.560 --> 00:03:59.320
Exactly.

00:03:59.320 --> 00:04:01.480
If a state has a lower per capita income,

00:04:01.480 --> 00:04:03.240
they get a higher matching rate.

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:05.840
It's designed to be this equalizing force, right?

00:04:05.840 --> 00:04:08.680
Ensuring that poorer states aren't just completely

00:04:08.680 --> 00:04:11.480
bankrupted by trying to provide basic health care

00:04:11.480 --> 00:04:12.920
to their lowest income residents.

00:04:12.920 --> 00:04:14.680
See, that makes logical sense to me.

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:17.360
It's a safety net that basically expands

00:04:17.360 --> 00:04:18.960
when the need is greater.

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:20.600
But from reading these source documents,

00:04:20.600 --> 00:04:22.080
that is not how it works in Puerto Rico.

00:04:22.080 --> 00:04:22.680
By at all.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:23.880
I was actually trying to visualize

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:25.680
the difference when I was reading through this.

00:04:25.680 --> 00:04:27.960
And it feels a bit like firefighting.

00:04:27.960 --> 00:04:31.160
So imagine a massive fire breaks out.

00:04:31.160 --> 00:04:33.560
If you are a state, the federal government

00:04:33.560 --> 00:04:36.360
hands you a garden hose, and they connect it directly

00:04:36.360 --> 00:04:38.200
to the municipal water main.

00:04:38.200 --> 00:04:38.560
Right.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:40.080
And as long as the fire is burning,

00:04:40.080 --> 00:04:42.760
meaning as long as you have sick, eligible people who

00:04:42.760 --> 00:04:45.560
need care, that water just keeps flowing,

00:04:45.560 --> 00:04:49.120
the supply is theoretically infinite as long as the need

00:04:49.120 --> 00:04:49.760
exists.

00:04:49.760 --> 00:04:52.360
That is a very, very accurate way to picture

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:53.760
the open-ended structure.

00:04:53.760 --> 00:04:55.280
But then you look at Puerto Rico,

00:04:55.280 --> 00:04:57.280
and they aren't given a garden hose.

00:04:57.280 --> 00:04:59.120
The federal government gives Puerto Rico

00:04:59.120 --> 00:05:01.760
a specific, totally predetermined number

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:03.320
of buckets of water.

00:05:03.320 --> 00:05:05.760
And they basically say, here is your water for the year.

00:05:05.760 --> 00:05:06.160
Yeah.

00:05:06.160 --> 00:05:06.760
Good luck.

00:05:06.760 --> 00:05:07.880
Exactly.

00:05:07.880 --> 00:05:09.400
And if the fire gets too big,

00:05:09.400 --> 00:05:12.000
or if the fire burns longer than expected,

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:14.320
those buckets eventually run dry.

00:05:14.320 --> 00:05:15.960
And once the buckets are empty, it just

00:05:15.960 --> 00:05:17.640
doesn't matter how big the fire is.

00:05:17.640 --> 00:05:19.760
It doesn't matter how many people are sick.

00:05:19.760 --> 00:05:21.640
The federal water completely stops.

00:05:21.640 --> 00:05:23.280
The federal water completely stops.

00:05:23.280 --> 00:05:24.520
And the local government is just

00:05:24.520 --> 00:05:26.800
left standing there with an empty bucket,

00:05:26.800 --> 00:05:29.120
completely responsible for every single dollar

00:05:29.120 --> 00:05:31.800
of health care costs beyond that point.

00:05:31.800 --> 00:05:34.120
And this capped structure, it's

00:05:34.120 --> 00:05:36.440
written into a very specific piece of the law.

00:05:36.440 --> 00:05:37.400
Which section is that?

00:05:37.400 --> 00:05:39.840
It's section 1108G of the Social Security Act.

00:05:39.880 --> 00:05:41.720
Section 1108G.

00:05:41.720 --> 00:05:42.760
OK.

00:05:42.760 --> 00:05:45.120
But it gets even worse than just having a cap, doesn't it?

00:05:45.120 --> 00:05:47.240
Let's talk about the actual size of the buckets

00:05:47.240 --> 00:05:48.040
they're given.

00:05:48.040 --> 00:05:50.800
Because you mentioned earlier that in the 50 states,

00:05:50.800 --> 00:05:53.080
the federal matching percentage, the FMAP,

00:05:53.080 --> 00:05:55.800
can go up to 83% for the poorest states.

00:05:55.800 --> 00:05:57.120
Right, up to 83.

00:05:57.120 --> 00:06:00.240
So where does Puerto Rico sit on that scale,

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:01.640
I mean, given their economy?

00:06:01.640 --> 00:06:05.080
Well, by permanent statute, the baseline law,

00:06:05.080 --> 00:06:07.640
as it's permanently written in the books right now,

00:06:07.680 --> 00:06:11.320
Puerto Rico's FMAP is locked at 55%.

00:06:11.320 --> 00:06:12.600
Yeah, just 55%.

00:06:12.600 --> 00:06:13.760
Just 55%.

00:06:13.760 --> 00:06:15.520
But Puerto Rico has a poverty rate

00:06:15.520 --> 00:06:19.520
that drastically exceeds literally any of the 50 states.

00:06:19.520 --> 00:06:20.960
If the whole point of the formula

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:23.360
is supposed to help poorer jurisdictions more,

00:06:23.360 --> 00:06:25.960
how on earth are they locked in at 55%.

00:06:25.960 --> 00:06:29.280
Because the standard formula just doesn't apply to them.

00:06:29.280 --> 00:06:31.640
If Puerto Rico were treated like a state,

00:06:31.640 --> 00:06:34.160
their per capita income is so low

00:06:34.160 --> 00:06:35.800
that it would automatically trigger

00:06:35.800 --> 00:06:38.000
the maximum allowable FMAP.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:40.000
So they'd be at the 83%.

00:06:40.000 --> 00:06:42.680
They would instantly be at that 83% tier.

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:44.440
But because they are a territory

00:06:44.440 --> 00:06:47.200
and they're operating under that section 1108G,

00:06:47.200 --> 00:06:50.880
the per capita income formula is essentially just bypassed.

00:06:50.880 --> 00:06:54.720
Congress simply hard-coded them into the law at 55%.

00:06:54.720 --> 00:06:56.880
So they get a capped number of buckets.

00:06:56.880 --> 00:06:58.880
And the buckets themselves are artificially small

00:06:58.880 --> 00:07:00.760
to begin with, totally ignoring

00:07:00.760 --> 00:07:03.280
the actual economic reality on the ground.

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:06.000
How does a healthcare system even function

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:07.200
under those kinds of conditions?

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:09.480
Honestly, functioning is probably too strong a word.

00:07:09.480 --> 00:07:10.320
Yeah.

00:07:10.320 --> 00:07:11.160
It survives.

00:07:11.160 --> 00:07:13.680
And it has really only survived over the last decade

00:07:13.680 --> 00:07:15.760
because Congress has been applying

00:07:15.760 --> 00:07:18.400
these temporary legislative band-aids.

00:07:18.400 --> 00:07:21.200
They haven't fixed the underlying structural inequity at all.

00:07:21.200 --> 00:07:24.520
They've just thrown extra temporary buckets of water

00:07:24.520 --> 00:07:26.360
at the island when the fire threatens

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:28.120
to burn the whole system down.

00:07:28.120 --> 00:07:29.680
Looking at the history in these documents,

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:33.080
it is just one exhausting cliffhanger after another.

00:07:33.080 --> 00:07:34.360
It seems like the local government

00:07:34.360 --> 00:07:36.120
can never actually plan for the future

00:07:36.120 --> 00:07:38.240
because they're always like six months away

00:07:38.240 --> 00:07:39.240
from running out of money.

00:07:39.240 --> 00:07:42.040
It's a devastating way to run a government agency.

00:07:42.040 --> 00:07:43.280
I mean, if we look back,

00:07:43.280 --> 00:07:46.080
the cycle of temporary patches really started in earnest

00:07:46.080 --> 00:07:47.720
with the Affordable Care Act.

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:51.080
Between July 2011 and December 2019,

00:07:51.080 --> 00:07:55.200
the ACA provided an additional $5.4 billion

00:07:55.200 --> 00:07:57.400
in Medicaid funding to Puerto Rico.

00:07:57.400 --> 00:07:59.200
But that had an expiration date.

00:07:59.200 --> 00:08:00.240
It did.

00:08:00.240 --> 00:08:02.320
And when that money started drying up,

00:08:02.320 --> 00:08:04.520
it triggered a totally new crisis.

00:08:04.520 --> 00:08:05.600
So Congress responded

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:08.800
with the Consolidated Appropriations Act of 2017,

00:08:08.800 --> 00:08:11.160
which threw in nearly $300 million.

00:08:11.160 --> 00:08:12.200
Right.

00:08:12.200 --> 00:08:15.360
Then, in the aftermath of the devastating hurricanes,

00:08:15.360 --> 00:08:18.680
they passed the Bipartisan Budget Act of 2018,

00:08:18.680 --> 00:08:21.440
which provided $3.6 billion

00:08:21.440 --> 00:08:24.080
in disaster response funding for healthcare.

00:08:24.080 --> 00:08:25.320
Always temporary.

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:27.640
And always disaster-driven, it seems like.

00:08:27.640 --> 00:08:28.800
Always.

00:08:28.800 --> 00:08:31.520
Which brings us to the most recent major patch,

00:08:31.560 --> 00:08:34.040
the one that was negotiated back in 2022.

00:08:34.040 --> 00:08:36.960
This is the Consolidated Appropriations Act of 2023,

00:08:36.960 --> 00:08:38.760
often just called the CAA.

00:08:38.760 --> 00:08:41.080
Right, I saw a lot about the CAA in the document.

00:08:41.080 --> 00:08:42.280
Yeah, this was a massive,

00:08:42.280 --> 00:08:44.200
incredibly complex relief package.

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:46.560
It was negotiated heavily by Jennifer Gonzalez,

00:08:46.560 --> 00:08:48.160
who was the resident commissioner at the time,

00:08:48.160 --> 00:08:49.640
and who is now the governor.

00:08:49.640 --> 00:08:52.200
So what did that 2022 deal actually do?

00:08:52.200 --> 00:08:54.200
Did it finally fix the bucket?

00:08:54.200 --> 00:08:55.840
Well, it made the bucket much bigger

00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:57.040
and it made the federal government pay

00:08:57.040 --> 00:08:58.080
for a lot more of the water,

00:08:58.080 --> 00:08:59.560
but again, only temporarily.

00:08:59.560 --> 00:09:00.400
Of course.

00:09:00.400 --> 00:09:05.200
The CAA provided roughly $19.4 billion to Puerto Rico,

00:09:05.200 --> 00:09:07.440
and that spread out over five years.

00:09:07.440 --> 00:09:12.240
It temporarily lifted that really punishing 55% FMAP

00:09:12.240 --> 00:09:13.480
up to 76%.

00:09:13.480 --> 00:09:14.600
Okay, that's a big jump.

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:15.440
Yeah, it is.

00:09:15.440 --> 00:09:17.680
And it provided a legally capped limit of federal funding

00:09:17.680 --> 00:09:19.720
that slowly scales up year over year,

00:09:19.720 --> 00:09:23.760
reaching about $3.8 billion by fiscal year 2027.

00:09:23.760 --> 00:09:27.120
But there is that word again, fiscal year 2027,

00:09:27.120 --> 00:09:28.440
the clock runs out.

00:09:28.440 --> 00:09:30.400
So if I'm trying to wrap my head around this

00:09:30.400 --> 00:09:31.920
from a legislative standpoint,

00:09:31.920 --> 00:09:35.160
I keep coming back to a really basic logistical question.

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:36.160
What's that?

00:09:36.160 --> 00:09:38.480
The US Congress is incredibly divided, right?

00:09:38.480 --> 00:09:40.280
It's highly polarized.

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:41.960
How do hundreds of lawmakers

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:44.600
who can barely agree on what day of the week it is

00:09:44.600 --> 00:09:46.360
establish a baseline of facts

00:09:46.360 --> 00:09:47.600
to even begin arguing

00:09:47.600 --> 00:09:49.840
about a territorial Medicaid formula?

00:09:49.840 --> 00:09:51.400
Who actually tells them what the math is?

00:09:51.400 --> 00:09:52.840
That is perhaps the most fascinating

00:09:52.840 --> 00:09:54.660
mechanical question in Washington.

00:09:54.660 --> 00:09:56.280
Because in a town where everyone has their own

00:09:56.320 --> 00:09:58.960
political spin and their own, you know, alternative math,

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:00.560
there is one entity that serves

00:10:00.560 --> 00:10:02.960
as the undisputed referee of reality.

00:10:02.960 --> 00:10:04.800
The Congressional Research Service.

00:10:04.800 --> 00:10:06.280
Yes, the CRS.

00:10:06.280 --> 00:10:09.140
And when it comes to territorial Medicaid funding,

00:10:09.140 --> 00:10:11.960
there is one specific author whose work is treated

00:10:11.960 --> 00:10:13.600
as the absolute gospel truth

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:15.480
by every single congressional office.

00:10:15.480 --> 00:10:17.240
And her name is Allison Mitchell.

00:10:17.240 --> 00:10:18.360
Her name is Allison Mitchell.

00:10:18.360 --> 00:10:20.040
I am so glad we're talking about this,

00:10:20.040 --> 00:10:22.000
because as a listener, you rarely hear

00:10:22.000 --> 00:10:24.440
about the actual people who write the documents

00:10:24.440 --> 00:10:27.200
that shape these multi-billion dollar decisions.

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:29.860
So what exactly is Allison Mitchell's role?

00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:32.400
Allison Mitchell is a specialist in healthcare financing

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:34.200
at the Congressional Research Service.

00:10:34.200 --> 00:10:36.680
And the CRS, they operate entirely

00:10:36.680 --> 00:10:38.680
within the Library of Congress.

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:41.700
Their mandate is incredibly vital to understand.

00:10:41.700 --> 00:10:42.880
They do not lobby.

00:10:42.880 --> 00:10:43.720
At all.

00:10:43.720 --> 00:10:44.560
Not at all.

00:10:44.560 --> 00:10:46.720
They do not advocate for policies.

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:49.120
They do not suggest what Congress should do.

00:10:49.120 --> 00:10:51.760
They simply serve as nonpartisan shared staff

00:10:51.760 --> 00:10:54.160
for congressional committees and members of Congress.

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:57.760
Their singular job is to provide factual, objective,

00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:00.280
legally sound descriptions of current law

00:11:00.280 --> 00:11:04.360
and how proposed changes would mathematically alter that law.

00:11:04.360 --> 00:11:07.160
So if a senator from Ohio and a representative

00:11:07.160 --> 00:11:09.280
from California are just screaming at each other

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:11.400
about how much Puerto Rico gets in Medicaid,

00:11:11.400 --> 00:11:13.560
they both have to look at Allison Mitchell's report

00:11:13.560 --> 00:11:15.640
to find out who is actually right.

00:11:15.640 --> 00:11:16.480
Precisely.

00:11:16.480 --> 00:11:18.120
Her reports are the baseline.

00:11:18.120 --> 00:11:20.360
If Puerto Rico wants to change the law,

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:22.520
they cannot walk into a committee hearing

00:11:22.520 --> 00:11:25.080
with like a glossy brochure

00:11:25.080 --> 00:11:26.920
and their own customized set of numbers.

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:28.440
They'd just be lashed out of the room.

00:11:28.440 --> 00:11:29.960
You would be dismissed immediately.

00:11:29.960 --> 00:11:32.040
If you want a divided Congress to act,

00:11:32.040 --> 00:11:34.680
you have to anchor your entire argument

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:37.440
to the CRS documentation of current law.

00:11:37.440 --> 00:11:39.400
Let's really dig into these specific documents

00:11:39.400 --> 00:11:42.280
because our sources indicate that Mitchell's PDFs

00:11:42.280 --> 00:11:43.600
are basically the holy grail

00:11:43.600 --> 00:11:46.040
for legislative staffers working on this issue.

00:11:46.040 --> 00:11:48.000
What is the first document they're looking at?

00:11:48.000 --> 00:11:50.200
So the primary document that every single

00:11:50.200 --> 00:11:52.120
legislative assistant keeps on their desk

00:11:52.160 --> 00:11:56.480
is IF-1112, specifically version 11,

00:11:56.480 --> 00:11:59.600
which was updated back in June of 2023.

00:11:59.600 --> 00:12:02.240
This is what the CRS calls an in-focus report.

00:12:02.240 --> 00:12:03.880
And this is just two pages, right?

00:12:03.880 --> 00:12:05.600
Exactly two pages.

00:12:05.600 --> 00:12:09.200
It is an absolute masterclass in concise policy writing.

00:12:09.200 --> 00:12:11.360
Like if you are a 24-year-old staffer

00:12:11.360 --> 00:12:14.120
who has exactly five minutes to brief a senator

00:12:14.120 --> 00:12:16.880
before they walk into a massive healthcare hearing,

00:12:16.880 --> 00:12:19.240
you read IF-111312.

00:12:19.280 --> 00:12:22.480
It distills the capped funding, the FMAP rate,

00:12:22.480 --> 00:12:25.480
the territorial exceptions, and the current funding levels

00:12:25.480 --> 00:12:27.800
into just one digestible brief.

00:12:27.800 --> 00:12:29.600
It is the cheat sheet that allows lawmakers

00:12:29.600 --> 00:12:31.120
to actually sound informed.

00:12:31.120 --> 00:12:33.360
But a two-page cheat sheet isn't gonna be enough

00:12:33.360 --> 00:12:35.480
if you are actually trying to write a new law

00:12:35.480 --> 00:12:38.440
or argue the deep historical context.

00:12:38.440 --> 00:12:40.080
What is the heavy artillery here?

00:12:40.080 --> 00:12:41.400
The heavy artillery was issued

00:12:41.400 --> 00:12:45.800
on the exact same day as that update, June 22, 2023.

00:12:45.800 --> 00:12:49.000
This is document R-47601, it's titled,

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:51.840
Legislative History of Medicaid Financing for the Territories.

00:12:51.840 --> 00:12:53.840
Which just sounds incredibly dense.

00:12:53.840 --> 00:12:57.080
Oh, it is incredibly dense, but it's so necessary.

00:12:57.080 --> 00:13:00.360
Because it tracks the exhaustive statute-by-statute history

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:02.240
from the open-ended inception of Medicaid

00:13:02.240 --> 00:13:03.720
all the way back in 1965

00:13:03.720 --> 00:13:05.880
up through every single temporary patch,

00:13:05.880 --> 00:13:08.280
disaster relief bill, and continuing resolution

00:13:08.280 --> 00:13:09.920
up to the 2023 CAA.

00:13:09.920 --> 00:13:11.200
So it's everything.

00:13:11.200 --> 00:13:13.800
It tracks every single dollar.

00:13:13.800 --> 00:13:15.720
If a fiscal conservative wants to argue

00:13:15.720 --> 00:13:16.840
that Puerto Rico has received

00:13:16.840 --> 00:13:18.960
too much temporary funding

00:13:18.960 --> 00:13:21.120
or if the Puerto Rican advocate wants to argue

00:13:21.120 --> 00:13:25.240
they've been historically short-changed by that 55% cap,

00:13:25.240 --> 00:13:29.440
both sides most cite R-47601 to make their case.

00:13:29.440 --> 00:13:30.920
It is the shared reality.

00:13:30.920 --> 00:13:32.560
Yes, but the document

00:13:32.560 --> 00:13:35.000
that is causing everyone to lose sleep right now,

00:13:35.000 --> 00:13:38.760
the document that sets the October 2027 trap door

00:13:38.760 --> 00:13:41.200
is R-49126.

00:13:41.200 --> 00:13:42.720
Expiring health provisions.

00:13:42.720 --> 00:13:43.560
Correct.

00:13:43.560 --> 00:13:46.360
This is the document where Mitchell explicitly flags

00:13:46.360 --> 00:13:48.120
the Puerto Rico Medicaid expiration

00:13:48.120 --> 00:13:50.040
as a must-act item for Congress.

00:13:50.040 --> 00:13:50.880
Wow.

00:13:50.880 --> 00:13:54.120
She meticulously lists the exact lines of statutory code

00:13:54.120 --> 00:13:55.240
that will simply vanish

00:13:55.240 --> 00:13:58.120
if Congress fails to pass new legislation.

00:13:58.120 --> 00:13:59.600
By putting this on the public record

00:13:59.600 --> 00:14:01.520
in an official CRS report,

00:14:01.520 --> 00:14:04.160
Mitchell ensures that Congress cannot claim ignorance.

00:14:04.160 --> 00:14:05.640
So no one can say they didn't know.

00:14:05.640 --> 00:14:06.480
Exactly.

00:14:06.480 --> 00:14:08.720
No lawmaker can wake up in November 2027

00:14:08.720 --> 00:14:09.920
and say, oh, I didn't realize

00:14:09.920 --> 00:14:11.400
the funding was gonna disappear.

00:14:11.400 --> 00:14:12.920
The CRS told him exactly when

00:14:12.920 --> 00:14:14.440
and how the trap door opens.

00:14:14.440 --> 00:14:16.160
All right, well, let's look down that trap door then.

00:14:16.160 --> 00:14:18.480
Let's look at the math that Mitchell has mapped out

00:14:18.480 --> 00:14:21.000
for October 1st, 2027.

00:14:21.000 --> 00:14:23.120
If we hit fiscal year 2028

00:14:23.120 --> 00:14:24.760
and a totally gridlocked Congress

00:14:24.760 --> 00:14:26.600
has done absolutely nothing,

00:14:26.600 --> 00:14:29.520
what exactly happens to the 1.3 million people

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:31.000
on Plan Vital?

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:33.080
So four very specific,

00:14:33.080 --> 00:14:37.040
very vital statutory provisions evaporate overnight.

00:14:37.040 --> 00:14:39.960
First, Social Security Act section 1108G.

00:14:39.960 --> 00:14:41.160
Okay, what is that one?

00:14:41.160 --> 00:14:42.000
This is the provision

00:14:42.000 --> 00:14:44.360
that temporarily elevated the funding cap.

00:14:44.360 --> 00:14:46.640
It is the provision that gave them the bigger bucket,

00:14:46.640 --> 00:14:49.720
allowing them to draw down roughly $3.8 billion

00:14:49.720 --> 00:14:52.640
in federal funds for fiscal year 2027.

00:14:52.640 --> 00:14:53.480
Gone.

00:14:53.480 --> 00:14:54.320
Gone.

00:14:54.320 --> 00:14:56.880
Second is section 1905 FF2.

00:14:56.880 --> 00:14:58.160
This is a provision that provided

00:14:58.160 --> 00:15:01.440
the temporary 76% FMAP matching rate.

00:15:01.440 --> 00:15:03.680
Without it, the match rate instantly drops back down

00:15:03.680 --> 00:15:06.640
to that punishing permanent law baseline of 55%.

00:15:06.640 --> 00:15:08.000
So the bucket is smaller

00:15:08.000 --> 00:15:09.760
and the local government suddenly has to pay

00:15:09.760 --> 00:15:11.880
a massive chunk of the water bill themselves

00:15:11.880 --> 00:15:13.480
all literally on the same day.

00:15:13.520 --> 00:15:15.200
Yes, it's a double hit.

00:15:15.200 --> 00:15:20.200
Third, sections 1108(g)(12) and (13) disappear.

00:15:20.200 --> 00:15:22.320
Now these were highly targeted provisions.

00:15:22.320 --> 00:15:24.880
They provided an extra $300 million

00:15:24.880 --> 00:15:28.120
specifically to establish a payment floor for physicians

00:15:28.120 --> 00:15:31.600
and an extra $75 million earmarked exclusively

00:15:31.600 --> 00:15:33.600
for program integrity efforts

00:15:33.600 --> 00:15:36.400
like fighting fraud and upgrading data systems.

00:15:36.400 --> 00:15:38.560
Okay, so the money that actually keeps doctors

00:15:38.560 --> 00:15:39.720
on the island and the money

00:15:39.720 --> 00:15:42.760
that proves the system isn't wasting funds,

00:15:42.760 --> 00:15:44.240
both of those vanished too.

00:15:44.240 --> 00:15:45.080
Completely.

00:15:45.080 --> 00:15:48.080
And the fourth line that expires is section 1108.

00:15:48.080 --> 00:15:50.160
This is the annual reporting requirement.

00:15:50.160 --> 00:15:51.440
It mandated that Puerto Rico

00:15:51.440 --> 00:15:53.880
submit extensive documentation to Congress

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:55.960
detailing exactly how they were spending the money.

00:15:55.960 --> 00:15:56.960
Which I mean, that sounds like

00:15:56.960 --> 00:15:58.640
just losing a paperwork burden,

00:15:58.640 --> 00:15:59.960
but from what we'll discuss later,

00:15:59.960 --> 00:16:02.080
that reporting requirement was actually Puerto Rico's

00:16:02.080 --> 00:16:04.520
best weapon to prove they were doing a good job.

00:16:04.520 --> 00:16:05.760
If that requirement vanishes,

00:16:05.760 --> 00:16:08.280
Congress just loses its window into the system.

00:16:08.280 --> 00:16:09.120
Exactly.

00:16:09.120 --> 00:16:10.480
Congress goes blind.

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:11.720
So when you put all four of those

00:16:11.720 --> 00:16:13.320
expiring provisions together,

00:16:13.320 --> 00:16:17.040
you trigger a terrifying statutory recalculation.

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:19.120
If Congress lets the clock run out,

00:16:19.120 --> 00:16:22.240
the funding for fiscal year 2028 is not calculated

00:16:22.240 --> 00:16:25.520
based on the $3.8 billion from 2027.

00:16:25.520 --> 00:16:27.560
Wait, if it isn't based on the previous year,

00:16:27.560 --> 00:16:28.600
what is it based on?

00:16:28.600 --> 00:16:31.720
By law, as strictly documented by the CRS,

00:16:31.720 --> 00:16:34.520
the funding formula reverts back to a calculation

00:16:34.520 --> 00:16:37.080
based on the fiscal year 2019 baseline.

00:16:37.080 --> 00:16:40.600
2019, but that is before the global pandemic.

00:16:40.640 --> 00:16:43.640
That's before years of massive medical inflation.

00:16:43.640 --> 00:16:47.520
That's before the entire 2022 funding deal even existed.

00:16:47.520 --> 00:16:49.800
It is a totally devastating reversion.

00:16:49.800 --> 00:16:51.680
If that happens, the federal cap to lot

00:16:51.680 --> 00:16:54.600
met plummets from roughly $3.8 billion

00:16:54.600 --> 00:16:57.400
down to approximately $500 million.

00:16:57.400 --> 00:16:58.320
I just want to repeat that

00:16:58.320 --> 00:16:59.680
so the listeners really grasp it.

00:16:59.680 --> 00:17:02.720
I dropped from $3.8 billion to $500 million.

00:17:02.720 --> 00:17:03.720
That isn't a budget cut.

00:17:03.720 --> 00:17:05.360
That is the complete obliteration

00:17:05.360 --> 00:17:06.320
of a healthcare system.

00:17:06.320 --> 00:17:07.680
It is, and the scary thing is,

00:17:07.680 --> 00:17:09.840
we don't have to guess about the impact

00:17:09.840 --> 00:17:11.840
because the Financial Oversight and Management Board

00:17:11.840 --> 00:17:14.880
for Puerto Rico, the FOMB, has already sounded the alarm.

00:17:14.880 --> 00:17:17.200
Right, the FOMB is the federal board

00:17:17.200 --> 00:17:19.200
that oversees the island's finances

00:17:19.200 --> 00:17:22.040
and actually certifies their budgets, right?

00:17:22.040 --> 00:17:22.880
Correct.

00:17:22.880 --> 00:17:26.440
On June 29, 2026, the FOMB certified

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:29.920
the fiscal year 2027 Commonwealth budget.

00:17:29.920 --> 00:17:32.200
And in that highly scrutinized document,

00:17:32.200 --> 00:17:34.480
they explicitly named the Medicaid cliff

00:17:34.480 --> 00:17:36.920
as one of the most material fiscal risks

00:17:36.920 --> 00:17:38.120
facing the entire island.

00:17:38.120 --> 00:17:39.040
Wow.

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:40.680
They noted that because Puerto Rico

00:17:40.680 --> 00:17:43.280
is subject to this rigid statutory cap,

00:17:43.280 --> 00:17:45.120
any healthcare expenditure that exceeds

00:17:45.120 --> 00:17:48.120
that $500 million limit in 2028

00:17:48.120 --> 00:17:49.880
would have to be financed entirely

00:17:49.880 --> 00:17:51.560
by the local government's general fund.

00:17:51.560 --> 00:17:53.040
And the local government doesn't just have

00:17:53.040 --> 00:17:55.320
an extra $3 billion lying around.

00:17:55.320 --> 00:17:56.480
They absolutely do not.

00:17:56.480 --> 00:17:57.920
The margins are so tight

00:17:57.920 --> 00:18:00.080
that the FOMB actually wrote control language

00:18:00.080 --> 00:18:01.560
into the budget resolution,

00:18:01.560 --> 00:18:03.280
requiring that any proposed changes

00:18:03.280 --> 00:18:06.440
to Medicaid benefits, literally any changes at all,

00:18:06.440 --> 00:18:08.880
have to be reviewed and approved by the FOMB first.

00:18:08.880 --> 00:18:11.240
So if you were an American citizen on Plan Vidal,

00:18:11.240 --> 00:18:13.880
what does that $500 million reversion look like for you?

00:18:13.880 --> 00:18:15.520
It means they have to kick hundreds of thousands

00:18:15.520 --> 00:18:17.600
of people off the eligibility rolls, right?

00:18:17.600 --> 00:18:19.360
It means slashing the benefits

00:18:19.360 --> 00:18:21.720
for the people who manage to remain.

00:18:21.720 --> 00:18:23.880
It means hospitals facing such massive

00:18:23.880 --> 00:18:25.880
uncompensated care costs

00:18:25.880 --> 00:18:28.080
that they simply have to close their doors.

00:18:28.080 --> 00:18:31.560
And without that $300 million physician payment floor,

00:18:31.560 --> 00:18:33.200
doctors and nurses will just pack up

00:18:33.200 --> 00:18:34.880
and leave the island for jobs on the mainland

00:18:34.880 --> 00:18:36.200
where they can actually make a living.

00:18:36.200 --> 00:18:38.680
Which brings us to a highly pragmatic

00:18:38.680 --> 00:18:42.440
incredibly cynical but very effective argument

00:18:42.440 --> 00:18:44.800
that is currently circulating in Washington, DC

00:18:44.800 --> 00:18:46.680
regarding this exact cliff.

00:18:46.680 --> 00:18:47.600
Okay, let's hear it.

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:51.360
It is about the mainland cost of this collapse.

00:18:51.360 --> 00:18:53.800
Because the 1.3 million people on Plan Vidal

00:18:53.800 --> 00:18:55.160
are American citizens.

00:18:55.160 --> 00:18:57.480
If the healthcare system on the island collapses,

00:18:57.480 --> 00:18:59.560
they don't just stop needing dialysis

00:18:59.560 --> 00:19:01.120
or insulin or cancer treatments.

00:19:01.120 --> 00:19:02.760
Right, they're citizens.

00:19:02.760 --> 00:19:03.960
They can just buy a plane ticket,

00:19:03.960 --> 00:19:06.240
land in Orlando or Philadelphia or New York

00:19:06.240 --> 00:19:07.840
and walk right into a hospital.

00:19:07.840 --> 00:19:08.920
Precisely.

00:19:08.920 --> 00:19:12.080
And when they migrate to the mainland United States,

00:19:12.080 --> 00:19:14.160
they enroll in the Medicaid programs

00:19:14.160 --> 00:19:16.000
of those specific states.

00:19:16.000 --> 00:19:19.760
And our sources highlight a startling economic reality

00:19:19.760 --> 00:19:22.440
that really changes the political calculus here.

00:19:22.440 --> 00:19:24.160
When a Puerto Rican citizen migrates

00:19:24.160 --> 00:19:26.160
to the mainland for health services,

00:19:26.160 --> 00:19:27.720
the cost to the federal government

00:19:27.720 --> 00:19:30.080
to treat them at standard state Medicaid rates

00:19:30.080 --> 00:19:33.360
is estimated at $165 million annually.

00:19:33.360 --> 00:19:34.880
Okay, $165 million.

00:19:34.880 --> 00:19:36.120
And now contrast that

00:19:36.120 --> 00:19:37.680
with what it costs the federal government

00:19:37.680 --> 00:19:39.640
to treat those exact same citizens

00:19:39.640 --> 00:19:40.960
if they stay on the island.

00:19:40.960 --> 00:19:43.200
That cost is roughly $44 million.

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:44.920
I wanna make sure I'm doing the math right here.

00:19:44.920 --> 00:19:47.400
It literally costs the federal government

00:19:47.400 --> 00:19:49.000
nearly four times as much money

00:19:49.000 --> 00:19:51.160
to force these citizens to migrate

00:19:51.160 --> 00:19:53.480
to Florida or Pennsylvania for care,

00:19:53.480 --> 00:19:55.400
rather than just adequately funding the system

00:19:55.400 --> 00:19:56.560
where they currently live.

00:19:56.560 --> 00:19:58.440
That is exactly what the data shows.

00:19:58.440 --> 00:20:01.320
From a purely fiscal mathematical standpoint,

00:20:01.320 --> 00:20:03.320
letting the Puerto Rican healthcare system collapse

00:20:03.320 --> 00:20:05.960
is a massive inefficiency for the federal taxpayer.

00:20:05.960 --> 00:20:07.440
That is mind boggling.

00:20:07.440 --> 00:20:10.240
But I imagine that is a very powerful tool

00:20:10.240 --> 00:20:12.760
for the Puerto Rican delegation in Congress

00:20:12.760 --> 00:20:15.080
because they can sit down with the fiscal hawk

00:20:15.080 --> 00:20:16.440
who wants to cut spending and say,

00:20:16.440 --> 00:20:18.800
look, if you drop us off this cliff,

00:20:18.800 --> 00:20:21.320
you are actually gonna spend four times as much money

00:20:21.320 --> 00:20:24.120
dealing with the fallout in your own home states.

00:20:24.120 --> 00:20:25.560
It is a profound argument.

00:20:25.560 --> 00:20:26.400
Yeah.

00:20:26.400 --> 00:20:27.840
It shakes the entire conversation

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:32.440
from territorial aid to domestic fiscal responsibility.

00:20:32.440 --> 00:20:35.080
Okay, I see the cliff, I see the CRS math,

00:20:35.080 --> 00:20:37.160
I see the terrible human and financial cost

00:20:37.160 --> 00:20:38.760
at the trap door opening.

