What one of the most technologically difficult hurdles

00:21:56.000 --> 00:21:59.640
was the mandate to implement an electronic asset tracker

00:21:59.640 --> 00:22:00.880
in bureaucratic terms,

00:22:00.880 --> 00:22:03.720
an asset verification system or AVS.

00:22:03.720 --> 00:22:05.200
What does that actually mean for the listener?

00:22:05.200 --> 00:22:06.040
Why is that hard?

00:22:06.040 --> 00:22:09.040
Historically, verifying someone's financial assets

00:22:09.040 --> 00:22:10.880
to see if they qualify for Medicaid

00:22:10.880 --> 00:22:14.160
was this highly manual, paper-based process.

00:22:14.160 --> 00:22:15.960
You either take the applicant's word for it

00:22:15.960 --> 00:22:18.640
or you wait for physical bank statements to arrive.

00:22:18.640 --> 00:22:21.000
Which takes forever and is prone to errors.

00:22:21.000 --> 00:22:22.080
Exactly.

00:22:22.080 --> 00:22:24.880
Congress demanded that Puerto Rico build a system

00:22:24.880 --> 00:22:28.600
that electronically and securely cross references

00:22:28.600 --> 00:22:30.560
and applicants stated income

00:22:30.560 --> 00:22:33.840
with financial institutions in real time to prevent fraud.

00:22:33.880 --> 00:22:35.920
It is a massive IT lift.

00:22:35.920 --> 00:22:37.440
It requires deep coordination

00:22:37.440 --> 00:22:39.560
between local government agencies, banks,

00:22:39.560 --> 00:22:40.840
and federal databases.

00:22:40.840 --> 00:22:42.160
And did they get it online?

00:22:42.160 --> 00:22:43.080
They did.

00:22:43.080 --> 00:22:46.040
According to the internal roadmap and federal filings,

00:22:46.040 --> 00:22:48.560
the federal centers for Medicare and Medicaid services

00:22:48.560 --> 00:22:50.480
officially approved their system plan

00:22:50.480 --> 00:22:52.640
on June 16th, 2026,

00:22:52.640 --> 00:22:56.280
with it becoming fully effective on January 1st, 2026.

00:22:56.280 --> 00:22:57.800
They check one of the biggest boxes

00:22:57.800 --> 00:22:59.640
Alison Mitchell had flagged in her report.

00:22:59.640 --> 00:23:00.480
That is huge.

00:23:00.480 --> 00:23:01.320
What about the doctors?

00:23:01.320 --> 00:23:03.560
We mentioned that extra $300 million

00:23:03.560 --> 00:23:05.560
was meant to establish a physician payment floor.

00:23:05.560 --> 00:23:07.040
Did that actually happen?

00:23:07.040 --> 00:23:07.880
It did.

00:23:07.880 --> 00:23:09.760
For years, Puerto Rico was basically

00:23:09.760 --> 00:23:11.160
hemorrhaging medical professionals

00:23:11.160 --> 00:23:13.440
because the local Medicaid reimbursement rates

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:15.880
were just so abysmal compared to the mainland,

00:23:15.880 --> 00:23:18.080
a doctor simply couldn't keep a clinic open.

00:23:18.080 --> 00:23:18.920
Right.

00:23:18.920 --> 00:23:21.000
So Congress mandated a floor

00:23:21.000 --> 00:23:23.120
and Puerto Rico successfully implemented

00:23:23.120 --> 00:23:24.720
a directed payment arrangement

00:23:24.720 --> 00:23:26.840
that permanently sets the reimbursement floor

00:23:26.840 --> 00:23:29.720
at 75% of the Medicare Part B rate.

00:23:29.720 --> 00:23:32.480
So a doctor treating a vulnerable patient

00:23:32.480 --> 00:23:35.120
on Plan Vital now has a predictable,

00:23:35.120 --> 00:23:37.280
sustainable baseline of income.

00:23:37.280 --> 00:23:38.120
Exactly.

00:23:38.120 --> 00:23:40.040
It stabilized the workforce.

00:23:40.040 --> 00:23:42.600
But the absolute centerpiece of their homework,

00:23:42.600 --> 00:23:44.200
the statistic that is gonna be printed

00:23:44.200 --> 00:23:46.320
on every single lobbying flyer handed out

00:23:46.320 --> 00:23:48.440
in Washington over the next 13 months,

00:23:48.440 --> 00:23:49.800
is their payment error rate.

00:23:49.800 --> 00:23:51.080
Okay, tell me about this error rate.

00:23:51.080 --> 00:23:52.000
How is it measured?

00:23:52.000 --> 00:23:53.480
It's called the PRM rate.

00:23:53.480 --> 00:23:54.960
It is a rigorous federal audit

00:23:54.960 --> 00:23:57.080
that measures improper payments in Medicaid

00:23:57.080 --> 00:23:59.040
across every state and territory.

00:23:59.040 --> 00:24:02.000
It measures everything from outright fraud to waste

00:24:02.000 --> 00:24:04.400
to simple administrative clerical mistakes.

00:24:04.400 --> 00:24:06.120
If you wanna know how tightly a jurisdiction

00:24:06.120 --> 00:24:07.640
runs its health care budget,

00:24:07.640 --> 00:24:09.080
you look at the PRM rate.

00:24:09.080 --> 00:24:11.280
So after all the historical concerns

00:24:11.280 --> 00:24:12.480
about mismanagement,

00:24:12.480 --> 00:24:15.280
what did Puerto Rico score on their federal audit?

00:24:15.280 --> 00:24:18.640
Puerto Rico's payment error rate came in at 2.51%.

00:24:18.640 --> 00:24:20.400
Okay, 2.51%.

00:24:20.400 --> 00:24:22.360
Out of context, that sounds low,

00:24:22.360 --> 00:24:23.480
but what's the baseline?

00:24:23.480 --> 00:24:25.720
How do the 50 states perform?

00:24:25.760 --> 00:24:29.360
The national average for the 50 states is 5.09%.

00:24:29.360 --> 00:24:30.200
Wait, hold on.

00:24:30.200 --> 00:24:31.160
Puerto Rico's error rate

00:24:31.160 --> 00:24:33.120
is less than half the national average.

00:24:33.120 --> 00:24:34.240
Yes.

00:24:34.240 --> 00:24:36.320
They are performing significantly better

00:24:36.320 --> 00:24:39.200
on payment integrity than the average US state.

00:24:39.200 --> 00:24:41.760
They are running a tighter ship than jurisdictions

00:24:41.760 --> 00:24:43.840
with vast open-ended funding

00:24:43.840 --> 00:24:45.800
and massive administrative budgets.

00:24:45.800 --> 00:24:47.240
How is that even possible?

00:24:47.240 --> 00:24:49.320
How does a territory in a perpetual state

00:24:49.320 --> 00:24:52.600
of fiscal crisis build a tighter anti-fraud system

00:24:52.600 --> 00:24:54.120
than wealthy mainland states?

00:24:54.120 --> 00:24:55.280
It wasn't an accident.

00:24:55.280 --> 00:24:56.560
And it certainly wasn't easy.

00:24:56.560 --> 00:24:58.440
If you look at the Medicaid annual reports

00:24:58.440 --> 00:25:01.720
from October 2021 through the recent updates,

00:25:01.720 --> 00:25:04.120
they detail a relentless structural overhaul

00:25:04.120 --> 00:25:05.360
of their internal plumbing.

00:25:05.360 --> 00:25:06.440
Give me an example of the plumbing.

00:25:06.440 --> 00:25:07.480
What did they change?

00:25:07.480 --> 00:25:10.360
They completely revolutionized their contracting.

00:25:10.360 --> 00:25:12.760
Historically, decentralized procurement

00:25:12.760 --> 00:25:15.800
can be a massive breeding ground for waste.

00:25:15.800 --> 00:25:17.120
They brought in centralized,

00:25:17.120 --> 00:25:20.040
highly competitive procurement processes.

00:25:20.040 --> 00:25:22.440
They implemented continuous improvement protocols

00:25:22.440 --> 00:25:24.360
for finding fraud and abuse.

00:25:24.360 --> 00:25:25.200
Okay.

00:25:25.200 --> 00:25:26.880
They set up incredibly strict,

00:25:26.880 --> 00:25:29.240
centralized data governance

00:25:29.240 --> 00:25:30.960
over the managed care companies

00:25:30.960 --> 00:25:33.480
that actually deliver the services to the patients.

00:25:33.480 --> 00:25:35.440
So they basically took away the ability

00:25:35.440 --> 00:25:36.800
for individual agencies

00:25:36.800 --> 00:25:38.680
to just hand out fragmented contracts.

00:25:38.680 --> 00:25:39.760
Exactly.

00:25:39.760 --> 00:25:40.720
The report even notes

00:25:40.720 --> 00:25:43.360
that they routed non-professional services,

00:25:43.360 --> 00:25:44.480
things like office cleaning

00:25:44.480 --> 00:25:46.760
and security for health department buildings,

00:25:46.760 --> 00:25:49.320
through the Puerto Rico General Services Administration

00:25:49.320 --> 00:25:51.840
to ensure forced competitive bidding.

00:25:51.840 --> 00:25:54.560
They tightened every single nut and bolt in the machine.

00:25:54.560 --> 00:25:56.280
And from a strategic standpoint,

00:25:56.280 --> 00:25:57.680
this just fundamentally changes

00:25:57.680 --> 00:25:59.560
the conversation in Washington.

00:25:59.560 --> 00:26:02.200
By doing this meticulous, documented homework,

00:26:02.200 --> 00:26:03.520
they have completely stripped away

00:26:03.520 --> 00:26:05.240
the traditional excuses.

00:26:05.240 --> 00:26:08.280
A lawmaker can no longer point to a lack of data

00:26:08.280 --> 00:26:09.840
or a high potential for fraud

00:26:09.840 --> 00:26:11.640
as a reason to deny funding.

00:26:11.640 --> 00:26:12.880
Precisely.

00:26:12.880 --> 00:26:15.600
Puerto Rico can now walk into a committee room,

00:26:15.600 --> 00:26:18.040
slide that 2.51% error rate

00:26:18.040 --> 00:26:19.960
and the approved electronic asset tracker

00:26:19.960 --> 00:26:20.880
across the table,

00:26:20.880 --> 00:26:23.240
and say, look, we manage our federal dollars

00:26:23.240 --> 00:26:25.400
better than half the states you represent.

00:26:25.400 --> 00:26:27.240
Now fund us like one.

00:26:27.240 --> 00:26:29.360
Okay, so if the homework is done

00:26:29.360 --> 00:26:31.240
and they have the data to prove it,

00:26:31.240 --> 00:26:33.360
what exactly is the demand?

00:26:33.360 --> 00:26:36.400
We know the cliff is looming in October, 2027.

00:26:36.400 --> 00:26:38.920
Are they just gonna ask for another five-year band aid

00:26:38.920 --> 00:26:40.760
to push the problem down the road again?

00:26:40.760 --> 00:26:41.680
Absolutely not.

00:26:41.680 --> 00:26:43.000
They are playing for keeps this time.

00:26:43.000 --> 00:26:45.400
They want a permanent structural fix.

00:26:45.400 --> 00:26:46.400
But to get that,

00:26:46.400 --> 00:26:47.840
they realized they had to fix

00:26:47.840 --> 00:26:50.440
a major political problem of their own first.

00:26:50.440 --> 00:26:51.360
What problem?

00:26:51.360 --> 00:26:52.520
Freelancing.

00:26:52.520 --> 00:26:53.840
What do you mean by freelancing?

00:26:53.840 --> 00:26:54.760
Well, in the past,

00:26:54.760 --> 00:26:56.840
when Puerto Rico needed federal funding,

00:26:56.840 --> 00:26:58.680
you would have the local hospital association

00:26:58.680 --> 00:27:00.200
asking for one thing,

00:27:00.200 --> 00:27:01.960
the pharmacies asking for something else,

00:27:01.960 --> 00:27:03.800
the local governor floating one number,

00:27:03.800 --> 00:27:06.040
and the representative in Washington

00:27:06.040 --> 00:27:07.960
negotiating a totally different number.

00:27:07.960 --> 00:27:09.920
And if you are a congressional staffer

00:27:09.920 --> 00:27:11.400
looking at four different demands

00:27:11.400 --> 00:27:12.600
from the same territory,

00:27:12.600 --> 00:27:13.440
you just throw your hands up

00:27:13.440 --> 00:27:15.280
and say, come back when you figure out what you want.

00:27:15.280 --> 00:27:16.160
Exactly.

00:27:16.160 --> 00:27:19.440
A fragmented ask is a dead ask in DC.

00:27:19.440 --> 00:27:21.360
So to prevent this,

00:27:21.360 --> 00:27:23.760
Governor Jennifer Gonzalez signed executive order

00:27:23.760 --> 00:27:28.520
2026-006 on February 5, 2026.

00:27:28.520 --> 00:27:30.880
What does the order actually do mechanically?

00:27:30.880 --> 00:27:34.040
It legally established a multi-sector working group.

00:27:34.040 --> 00:27:36.520
This is not just a bunch of bureaucrats in a back room.

00:27:36.520 --> 00:27:39.280
It mandates that the local Department of Health,

00:27:39.280 --> 00:27:41.040
the fiscal advisory agencies,

00:27:41.040 --> 00:27:42.680
the hospital associations,

00:27:42.680 --> 00:27:44.640
the medical associations, the pharmacies,

00:27:44.640 --> 00:27:45.600
and crucially,

00:27:45.600 --> 00:27:47.760
the resident commissioner in Washington,

00:27:47.760 --> 00:27:50.160
currently, Pablo Jose Hernandez Herrera,

00:27:50.160 --> 00:27:52.000
all have to sit at the exact same table.

00:27:52.000 --> 00:27:54.760
They literally locked the entire healthcare ecosystem

00:27:54.760 --> 00:27:56.480
in a room and told them they couldn't leave

00:27:56.480 --> 00:27:57.880
until they agreed on one number.

00:27:57.880 --> 00:27:58.840
Yes.

00:27:58.840 --> 00:28:00.680
The executive order explicitly aims

00:28:00.680 --> 00:28:02.440
to create a single voice.

00:28:02.440 --> 00:28:04.520
No one goes to DC in freelances.

00:28:04.520 --> 00:28:06.280
They present a unified strategy.

00:28:06.280 --> 00:28:08.640
So what did the single voice decide to ask for?

00:28:08.640 --> 00:28:10.160
Let's talk about tier A,

00:28:10.160 --> 00:28:11.880
the absolute best case scenario.

00:28:11.880 --> 00:28:13.840
Tier A is structural parity.

00:28:13.840 --> 00:28:15.120
It is the holy grail.

00:28:15.120 --> 00:28:17.760
They want Congress to completely repeal

00:28:17.760 --> 00:28:21.000
the Section 1108G funding cap for Puerto Rico.

00:28:21.000 --> 00:28:23.200
Throw away the buckets, give them the garden hose.

00:28:23.200 --> 00:28:24.040
Exactly.

00:28:24.040 --> 00:28:27.240
Make the funding open-ended, just like the 50 states.

00:28:27.240 --> 00:28:29.120
And alongside repealing the cap,

00:28:29.120 --> 00:28:31.440
they want to secure a permanent matching rate,

00:28:31.440 --> 00:28:33.760
an FMAP of 83%.

00:28:33.760 --> 00:28:36.040
Based on the actual poverty levels of the island.

00:28:36.040 --> 00:28:36.880
Yes.

00:28:36.880 --> 00:28:39.760
And there is a profound inherent fairness argument

00:28:39.760 --> 00:28:41.120
they are leveraging here.

00:28:41.120 --> 00:28:42.680
It is an argument that is very hard

00:28:42.680 --> 00:28:44.240
for Congress to refute.

00:28:44.240 --> 00:28:47.440
When Congress passed that massive 2022 deal,

00:28:47.440 --> 00:28:49.800
they didn't just give temporary help to Puerto Rico.

00:28:49.800 --> 00:28:52.000
They actually permanently fixed the FMAP

00:28:52.000 --> 00:28:53.680
for the other four US territories.

00:28:53.680 --> 00:28:54.520
Wait, really?

00:28:54.520 --> 00:28:58.160
American Samoa, Guam, the Northern Mariana Islands,

00:28:58.160 --> 00:28:59.800
and the US Virgin Islands, they all got it.

00:28:59.800 --> 00:29:00.640
Yes.

00:29:00.640 --> 00:29:01.960
All four of those territories were granted

00:29:01.960 --> 00:29:06.080
a permanent 83% FMAP in that 2022 legislation.

00:29:06.080 --> 00:29:07.800
Puerto Rico is the sole outlier.

00:29:07.800 --> 00:29:08.760
That is wild.

00:29:08.760 --> 00:29:10.120
So as the governor noted

00:29:10.120 --> 00:29:12.320
at the recent health policy conference,

00:29:12.320 --> 00:29:14.040
they are literally just asking,

00:29:14.040 --> 00:29:16.840
why is the statutory math applied to Puerto Rico

00:29:16.840 --> 00:29:18.880
less favorable than the math applied

00:29:18.880 --> 00:29:20.320
to the US Virgin Islands?

00:29:20.320 --> 00:29:21.520
They just want the same treatment

00:29:21.520 --> 00:29:22.440
as the other territories?

00:29:22.440 --> 00:29:23.280
Exactly.

00:29:23.280 --> 00:29:24.120
It is a demand for equity

00:29:24.120 --> 00:29:26.720
within the territorial system itself.

00:29:26.720 --> 00:29:29.160
But because they know Washington is deeply resistant

00:29:29.160 --> 00:29:31.120
to open-ended entitlements,

00:29:31.120 --> 00:29:34.440
they developed a locked specific fallback demand.

00:29:34.440 --> 00:29:37.440
The May, 2026 fallback, what is it?

00:29:37.440 --> 00:29:39.520
If open-ended parity fails,

00:29:39.520 --> 00:29:40.920
the unified fallback demand

00:29:40.920 --> 00:29:43.120
is an initial capped allocation

00:29:43.120 --> 00:29:48.120
of $4.415 billion for fiscal year 2028.

00:29:48.320 --> 00:29:50.280
And it comes with a statutory guarantee

00:29:50.280 --> 00:29:52.240
that it grows by 5% annually

00:29:52.240 --> 00:29:54.000
to account for medical inflation,

00:29:54.000 --> 00:29:57.040
combined with a permanent 83% FMAP.

00:29:57.040 --> 00:29:58.280
Okay, so the pieces are on the board.

00:29:58.280 --> 00:29:59.100
They have the homework.

00:29:59.100 --> 00:30:00.840
They have the 2.51 error rate.

00:30:00.840 --> 00:30:02.000
They have the unified ask

00:30:02.000 --> 00:30:04.120
and they know exactly what Alison Mitchell's baseline

00:30:04.120 --> 00:30:05.400
says they have to overcome.

00:30:05.400 --> 00:30:06.720
The million dollar question,

00:30:06.720 --> 00:30:08.760
or rather the multi-billion dollar question,

00:30:08.760 --> 00:30:10.600
is how do they actually execute this?

00:30:10.600 --> 00:30:12.080
We know what they want.

00:30:12.080 --> 00:30:13.440
But how do they navigate

00:30:13.440 --> 00:30:16.440
a polarized, slow-moving Congress

00:30:16.440 --> 00:30:19.680
to pass this before the trapdoor opens in 13 months?

00:30:19.680 --> 00:30:22.160
This is where we transition from policy analysis

00:30:22.160 --> 00:30:25.640
to pure legislative tactical warfare.

00:30:25.640 --> 00:30:27.640
The internal roadmap documents

00:30:27.640 --> 00:30:30.360
detail a meticulously-calendered,

00:30:30.360 --> 00:30:33.080
step-by-step operational strategy.

00:30:33.080 --> 00:30:34.960
They have converted the statutory math

00:30:34.960 --> 00:30:36.760
into a four-phase war plan.

00:30:36.760 --> 00:30:38.160
Okay, let's break down the calendar.

00:30:38.160 --> 00:30:39.120
Phase one is right now.

00:30:39.120 --> 00:30:40.360
It runs through the end of this month,

00:30:40.360 --> 00:30:42.200
September 30, 2026.

00:30:42.200 --> 00:30:43.920
What is the immediate mission?

00:30:43.920 --> 00:30:46.680
The immediate mission is stability and table setting.

00:30:46.680 --> 00:30:48.080
They must secure and confirm

00:30:48.080 --> 00:30:50.360
the fiscal year 2026 certifications

00:30:50.360 --> 00:30:52.400
for that extra 300 million for doctors

00:30:52.400 --> 00:30:54.440
and 75 million for integrity.

00:30:54.440 --> 00:30:55.400
If they let those lapse,

00:30:55.400 --> 00:30:56.800
the system starts bleeding early.

00:30:56.800 --> 00:30:57.640
Right.

00:30:57.640 --> 00:30:59.160
Second, they must physically file

00:30:59.160 --> 00:31:01.520
that section 1108 annual report on time.

00:31:01.520 --> 00:31:02.760
They have to turn the homework in.

00:31:02.760 --> 00:31:04.000
They turn the homework in,

00:31:04.000 --> 00:31:05.720
proving the low error rate.

00:31:05.720 --> 00:31:08.040
Then they take their two-page summary,

00:31:08.080 --> 00:31:10.400
attach Allison Mitchell's CRS PDFs

00:31:10.400 --> 00:31:11.760
to prove the baseline,

00:31:11.760 --> 00:31:13.280
attach the governor's executive order

00:31:13.280 --> 00:31:14.640
to prove they are unified,

00:31:14.640 --> 00:31:17.360
and they physically deliver these packets

00:31:17.360 --> 00:31:19.840
to the staffs of the key committees,

00:31:19.840 --> 00:31:21.560
energy and commerce in the house,

00:31:21.560 --> 00:31:22.920
and finance in the Senate.

00:31:22.920 --> 00:31:25.120
Now, I noticed a very specific,

00:31:25.120 --> 00:31:28.400
bolded tactical note in the roadmap for this phase.

00:31:28.400 --> 00:31:29.880
It says, do not hang this

00:31:29.880 --> 00:31:32.160
on the October continuing resolution.

00:31:32.160 --> 00:31:33.880
Why would they avoid that?

00:31:33.880 --> 00:31:35.760
A continuing resolution, a CR,

00:31:35.760 --> 00:31:38.480
is a must pass bill to keep the government open.

00:31:38.480 --> 00:31:39.800
If they need this fix,

00:31:39.800 --> 00:31:40.680
shouldn't they attach it

00:31:40.680 --> 00:31:42.560
to whatever train is leaving the station?

00:31:42.560 --> 00:31:45.200
It is a brilliant piece of legislative restraint.

00:31:45.200 --> 00:31:46.760
You have to understand the chaos

00:31:46.760 --> 00:31:49.040
of a pre-election continuing resolution.

00:31:49.040 --> 00:31:51.880
It is a messy last minute stopgap measure

00:31:51.880 --> 00:31:54.440
designed solely to prevent a government shutdown

00:31:54.440 --> 00:31:56.280
right before voters go to the polls.

00:31:56.280 --> 00:31:57.240
Everyone is panicking.

00:31:57.240 --> 00:31:58.640
Right, it's just keeping the lights on.

00:31:58.640 --> 00:31:59.520
Exactly.

00:31:59.520 --> 00:32:02.200
If you try to jam a highly complex,

00:32:02.200 --> 00:32:04.680
multi-billion dollar structural rewrite

00:32:04.680 --> 00:32:07.480
of territorial Medicaid into that chaotic environment,

00:32:07.480 --> 00:32:08.920
one of two things will happen.

00:32:08.920 --> 00:32:10.360
Either it gets stripped out entirely

00:32:10.360 --> 00:32:12.800
because it's too complicated for a quick vote,

00:32:12.800 --> 00:32:16.800
or worse, you panic and accept a terrible short-term patch

00:32:16.800 --> 00:32:17.880
just to get something.

00:32:17.880 --> 00:32:18.720
Oh, I see.

00:32:18.720 --> 00:32:20.320
And if you accept a six-month patch,

00:32:20.320 --> 00:32:22.680
you ruin your momentum for a permanent fix.

00:32:22.680 --> 00:32:25.240
The strategy is to keep their powder dry

00:32:25.240 --> 00:32:27.360
and wait for a proper legislative vehicle.

00:32:27.360 --> 00:32:28.440
Okay, that makes total sense.

00:32:28.440 --> 00:32:31.120
So they survive the fall, they don't jump on the CR.

00:32:31.120 --> 00:32:32.720
That brings us to phase two.

00:32:32.720 --> 00:32:36.360
October 2026 through January 3, 2027.

00:32:36.360 --> 00:32:38.120
This is the post-election period.

00:32:38.120 --> 00:32:40.120
The infamous lame duck session.

00:32:40.120 --> 00:32:42.000
Why is a lame duck session so unique

00:32:42.000 --> 00:32:43.720
for this kind of strategy?

00:32:43.720 --> 00:32:45.880
Because half the room just lost their elections

00:32:45.880 --> 00:32:47.400
or they are retiring.

00:32:47.400 --> 00:32:49.920
The political pressure is suddenly very different.

00:32:49.920 --> 00:32:51.760
The roadmap dictates that Puerto Rico

00:32:51.760 --> 00:32:55.640
needs to have highly detailed legislative discussion drafts

00:32:55.640 --> 00:32:58.760
circulating among committee staff before Thanksgiving.

00:32:58.760 --> 00:33:01.560
They want to attempt to get a lame duck bridge passed,

00:33:01.560 --> 00:33:04.600
perhaps a strong down payment on their ultimate ask,

00:33:04.600 --> 00:33:08.120
negotiated by members who no longer fear primary challengers.

00:33:08.120 --> 00:33:10.760
And this is where that single voice executive order

00:33:10.760 --> 00:33:12.400
is truly tested, right?

00:33:12.400 --> 00:33:13.640
The roadmap says the governor

00:33:13.640 --> 00:33:16.520
and the resident commissioner must appear together in DC,

00:33:16.520 --> 00:33:18.480
repeating the exact same number.

00:33:18.480 --> 00:33:21.160
If there is even a sliver of daylight between them,

00:33:21.160 --> 00:33:23.720
committee staffers will exploit it to delay the bill.

00:33:23.720 --> 00:33:24.960
Exactly.

00:33:24.960 --> 00:33:27.200
And crucially, they must keep Medicaid

00:33:27.200 --> 00:33:30.200
entirely separated from the political status debate.

00:33:30.200 --> 00:33:32.320
The statehood versus independence debate.

00:33:32.320 --> 00:33:33.240
Yes.

00:33:33.240 --> 00:33:36.040
Whether a lawmaker supports statehood, independence,

00:33:36.040 --> 00:33:37.680
or maintaining the commonwealth,

00:33:37.680 --> 00:33:39.560
the roadmap demands that Medicaid funding

00:33:39.560 --> 00:33:43.640
be treated as an isolated life or death civic necessity.

00:33:43.640 --> 00:33:46.400
If it gets tangled in the status debate, it dies.

00:33:46.400 --> 00:33:50.280
All right, phase three, January to April, 2027,

00:33:50.280 --> 00:33:53.040
the new Congress is seated and sworn in.

00:33:53.040 --> 00:33:54.600
The clock is really ticking now.

00:33:54.600 --> 00:33:56.120
This is the heavy lifting phase.

00:33:56.120 --> 00:33:58.520
This is when you push for formal committee markups

00:33:58.520 --> 00:33:59.760
in public hearings.

00:33:59.800 --> 00:34:02.280
This is when you force the congressional budget office,

00:34:02.280 --> 00:34:04.960
the CBO, to officially score the cost

00:34:04.960 --> 00:34:06.320
of the different funding tiers.

00:34:06.320 --> 00:34:08.880
You make the math official on the congressional record.

00:34:08.880 --> 00:34:09.720
Yes.

00:34:09.720 --> 00:34:13.680
And this is when you deploy the heavy artillery, the FOMB.

00:34:13.680 --> 00:34:16.160
The strategy calls for the financial oversight board

00:34:16.160 --> 00:34:19.680
to submit a formal stark letter to congressional leadership,

00:34:19.680 --> 00:34:23.680
clearly stating that a $3.5 billion Medicaid cut

00:34:23.680 --> 00:34:26.680
will fundamentally break Puerto Rico's certified budgets.

00:34:26.680 --> 00:34:28.480
That is a brilliant pressure point.

00:34:28.520 --> 00:34:30.040
Congress created the FOMB.

00:34:30.040 --> 00:34:31.280
Congress told Puerto Rico,

00:34:31.280 --> 00:34:33.600
you must balance your budgets under our oversight.

00:34:33.600 --> 00:34:36.240
So Puerto Rico uses the federal fiscal watchdogs

00:34:36.240 --> 00:34:37.520
against Congress itself.

00:34:37.520 --> 00:34:39.200
They say, we followed your rules,

00:34:39.200 --> 00:34:41.280
but we mathematically cannot balance the budget

00:34:41.280 --> 00:34:43.520
if you drop this $3 billion cliff on us.

00:34:43.520 --> 00:34:44.840
You have to fix it.

00:34:44.840 --> 00:34:47.040
It is using the architecture of federal control

00:34:47.040 --> 00:34:48.480
to force federal action.

00:34:48.480 --> 00:34:50.120
Which brings us to phase four,

00:34:50.120 --> 00:34:52.800
May to September 30, 2027.

00:34:52.800 --> 00:34:54.440
The trapdoor is opening.

00:34:54.440 --> 00:34:55.600
The danger zone.

00:34:55.600 --> 00:34:57.400
If there is no standalone parity bill

00:34:57.400 --> 00:34:59.680
moving through the chambers by Memorial Day,

00:34:59.680 --> 00:35:01.560
the strategy fundamentally shifts.

00:35:01.560 --> 00:35:04.480
You abandon the dream of a beautiful, clean,

00:35:04.480 --> 00:35:06.200
standalone parity act.

00:35:06.200 --> 00:35:07.800
You shift into survival mode

00:35:07.800 --> 00:35:10.920
and you look for a massive vehicle to attach yourself to.

00:35:10.920 --> 00:35:12.320
You attempt to attach a rider

00:35:12.320 --> 00:35:15.680
to the massive must pass September vehicle.

00:35:15.680 --> 00:35:19.880
The roadmap refers to this as the year end extender bundle.

00:35:19.880 --> 00:35:21.280
I want to push back on this strategy

00:35:21.280 --> 00:35:23.240
because looking at what goes into this bundle,

00:35:23.240 --> 00:35:24.400
it is massive.

00:35:24.400 --> 00:35:27.720
It includes delayed disproportionate share hospital cuts

00:35:27.720 --> 00:35:28.800
for mainland states.

00:35:28.800 --> 00:35:30.760
The money follows the person program,

00:35:30.760 --> 00:35:32.960
billions in community health center funds.

00:35:32.960 --> 00:35:35.720
There are dozens of massive national healthcare provisions

00:35:35.720 --> 00:35:36.560
in this pile.

00:35:36.560 --> 00:35:37.400
There are, yeah.

00:35:37.400 --> 00:35:39.640
Given that Puerto Rico is asking for billions of dollars

00:35:39.640 --> 00:35:40.960
in a structural rewrite,

00:35:40.960 --> 00:35:43.760
doesn't getting bundled into this massive extender pile

00:35:43.760 --> 00:35:46.080
risk getting completely lost in the noise.

00:35:46.080 --> 00:35:48.480
It is a massive tactical gamble,

00:35:48.480 --> 00:35:52.200
but it is often the only way things get done in DC.

00:35:52.240 --> 00:35:54.000
Being a standalone bill means

00:35:54.000 --> 00:35:57.120
you are a singular isolated target.

00:35:57.120 --> 00:35:59.160
If a senator from a fifthly conservative state

00:35:59.160 --> 00:36:01.600
wants to make a point about federal spending,

00:36:01.600 --> 00:36:05.160
they can easily vote against a standalone bill for Puerto Rico.

00:36:05.160 --> 00:36:07.040
It costs them nothing politically at home.

00:36:07.040 --> 00:36:08.160
Right, they can just say

00:36:08.160 --> 00:36:09.640
they are saving taxpayer money.

00:36:09.640 --> 00:36:10.680
Exactly.

00:36:10.680 --> 00:36:13.280
But being embedded in the extender pile

00:36:13.280 --> 00:36:15.840
provides a sort of legislative herd immunity.

00:36:15.840 --> 00:36:18.160
Herd immunity that perfectly describes it.

00:36:18.160 --> 00:36:20.280
If that same senator wants to vote against

00:36:20.280 --> 00:36:22.240
Puerto Rico's Medicaid funding,

00:36:22.240 --> 00:36:24.120
but doing so means they also have to vote against

00:36:24.120 --> 00:36:25.480
funding the community health centers

00:36:25.480 --> 00:36:27.480
in their own home state,

00:36:27.480 --> 00:36:30.120
they will hold their nose, complain about the process,

00:36:30.120 --> 00:36:32.360
and vote yes on the entire bundle.

00:36:32.360 --> 00:36:34.920
The extender bundle is how Washington passes

00:36:34.920 --> 00:36:36.680
necessary things that nobody wants to take

00:36:36.680 --> 00:36:38.280
a tough individual vote on.

00:36:38.280 --> 00:36:39.440
It provides camouflage.

00:36:39.440 --> 00:36:41.560
So if they are in that extender pile,

00:36:41.560 --> 00:36:45.320
bargaining at the absolute 11th hour in September, 2027,

00:36:45.320 --> 00:36:47.360
and structural parity is off the table,

00:36:47.360 --> 00:36:49.040
what is the absolute floor?

00:36:49.040 --> 00:36:51.840
What is the minimum acceptable rider they will agree to?

00:36:51.840 --> 00:36:54.240
The minimum survival package according to the strategy

00:36:54.240 --> 00:36:57.400
is freezing the fiscal year 2027 funding levels,

00:36:57.400 --> 00:36:59.520
adding a mandatory bump for medical inflation

00:36:59.520 --> 00:37:01.800
so the money doesn't lose its purchasing power,

00:37:01.800 --> 00:37:05.520
and locking the FMAP at either 76 or 82%

00:37:05.520 --> 00:37:07.040
for at least two more years.

00:37:07.040 --> 00:37:09.440
So it's just another bucket, another Band-Aid.

00:37:09.440 --> 00:37:12.480
It is, but it stops the trapdoor from opening.

00:37:12.480 --> 00:37:14.440
It prevents that catastrophic reversion

00:37:14.440 --> 00:37:18.120
to the 2019 baseline of $500 million.

00:37:18.160 --> 00:37:20.560
It keeps the system alive to fight another day.

00:37:20.560 --> 00:37:23.520
Wow, we have covered an immense amount of ground today.

00:37:23.520 --> 00:37:25.520
Let's take a breath and do a quick recap.

00:37:25.520 --> 00:37:26.800
We are looking at a scenario

00:37:26.800 --> 00:37:28.360
with incredibly high stakes.

00:37:28.360 --> 00:37:29.280
Incredibly high.

00:37:29.280 --> 00:37:31.800
1.3 million American citizens

00:37:31.800 --> 00:37:33.920
whose access to basic healthcare relies

00:37:33.920 --> 00:37:37.040
on a rigid, outdated statutory formula.

00:37:37.040 --> 00:37:40.160
They are facing a catastrophic multi-billion dollar drop

00:37:40.160 --> 00:37:41.600
in exactly 13 months

00:37:41.600 --> 00:37:44.280
because of how a specific section of the law is written,

00:37:44.280 --> 00:37:46.000
but they aren't just waiting for it to happen.

00:37:46.000 --> 00:37:48.640
They have executed a massive administrative overhaul,

00:37:48.640 --> 00:37:51.440
achieving a 2.51% error rate

00:37:51.440 --> 00:37:53.040
that beats the national average.

00:37:53.040 --> 00:37:55.040
They have formed a unified political front,

00:37:55.040 --> 00:37:56.920
and they are using the objective,

00:37:56.920 --> 00:38:00.000
unassailable math, CRS experts like Alison Mitchell

00:38:00.000 --> 00:38:03.320
to execute a 13-month legislative war plan.

00:38:03.320 --> 00:38:05.920
And if we step back and ask the ultimate question,

00:38:05.920 --> 00:38:07.280
you know, so what?

00:38:07.280 --> 00:38:09.280
Why does this matter to someone listening in Ohio

00:38:09.280 --> 00:38:10.960
or Oregon or Maine?

00:38:10.960 --> 00:38:14.200
It matters because this is a profound real-time case study

00:38:14.200 --> 00:38:15.640
in how federal statutory math

00:38:15.640 --> 00:38:18.200
can literally make or break an entire regional economy.

00:38:18.200 --> 00:38:19.040
Right.

00:38:19.040 --> 00:38:21.680
Understanding the dry, highly technical work

00:38:21.680 --> 00:38:23.040
of people like Alison Mitchell,

00:38:23.040 --> 00:38:25.080
the baseline formulas, the capped allotments,

00:38:25.080 --> 00:38:26.360
the expiration dates,

00:38:26.360 --> 00:38:28.000
that is the secret key to understanding

00:38:28.000 --> 00:38:30.280
how American governance actually operates.

00:38:30.280 --> 00:38:31.600
The true power in Washington

00:38:31.600 --> 00:38:33.600
isn't in the speeches on the floor, you know?

00:38:33.600 --> 00:38:36.320
It is in the math buried in those CRS PDFs.

00:38:36.320 --> 00:38:38.200
It is entirely about the size of the bucket

00:38:38.200 --> 00:38:39.240
and who controls the water.

00:38:39.240 --> 00:38:40.240
That's exactly.

00:38:40.240 --> 00:38:41.600
Well, I want to leave everyone listening

00:38:41.600 --> 00:38:44.160
with a final, provocative thought to mull over.

00:38:44.160 --> 00:38:45.640
Something that builds on everything

00:38:45.640 --> 00:38:46.640
we've discussed today

00:38:46.640 --> 00:38:50.040
about the realities of policy and human behavior.

00:38:50.040 --> 00:38:52.880
Let's imagine the absolute worst-case scenario.

00:38:52.880 --> 00:38:54.680
Let's say the strategy fails.

00:38:54.680 --> 00:38:56.480
Congress gets completely gridlocked,

00:38:56.480 --> 00:38:58.280
the extender pile collapses,

00:38:58.280 --> 00:39:02.080
and the October 2027 trapdoor opens.

00:39:02.080 --> 00:39:05.520
Funding plummets to $500 million.

00:39:05.520 --> 00:39:06.480
The system breaks.

00:39:06.480 --> 00:39:07.560
The system breaks.

00:39:07.560 --> 00:39:08.600
And as we discussed,

00:39:08.600 --> 00:39:10.560
that means hundreds of thousands of Puerto Ricans

00:39:10.560 --> 00:39:12.160
who are American citizens

00:39:12.160 --> 00:39:14.480
might be forced to relocate to the U.S. mainland

00:39:14.480 --> 00:39:16.840
simply to access lifesaving medical care

00:39:16.840 --> 00:39:18.920
that they can no longer get on the island.

00:39:18.920 --> 00:39:20.280
If that migration happens,

00:39:20.280 --> 00:39:22.080
if hundreds of thousands of citizens

00:39:22.080 --> 00:39:25.240
